Thanks. Merged and cherry-picked https://github.com/apache/kafka/pull/23341




I was also recently looking into 
https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-20939 which was a blocker, too.

Wondering if disabling KIP-909 behavior on the clients by default is 
sufficient? -- Both Kafka Connect and Kafka Streams, expose all client 
config so users could enable this feature. I did look into both cases.

For KS, it seems overall to be "safe", ie, nothing really bad would 
happen if a user enables KIP-909, but there is also no benefit. The goal 
of KIP-909 is to be more resilient against DNS changes, but KS would 
just crash with an error and shut down hitting the new exception.

However, for KC it seems to be problematic, as KC could enter a bad 
state it cannot recover from, and it does not error out cleanly either. 
-- I am not familiar with Connect and can only parrot what Claude spit 
out. Might be worth to double check by someone familiar with Connect?

Maybe we should remove the new config from both KS and KC as quick fix 
for 4.4 release, and do proper follow-up work on each to make both 
KP-909 ready?

Thoughts?



-Matthias




