 an off-by-one in non-atomic batch appending
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: quoted-printable
X-JIRA-FingerPrint: 30527f35849b9dde25b450d4833f0394
From: "ChenXi (Jira)" <jira@apache.org>
Reply-To: <dev@kafka.apache.org>

ChenXi created KAFKA-21129:
------------------------------

             Summary: KRaft controller failover caused by an off-by-one in =
non-atomic batch appending
                 Key: KAFKA-21129
                 URL: https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/KAFKA-21129
             Project: Kafka
          Issue Type: Bug
          Components: controller
    Affects Versions: 4.3.1
            Reporter: ChenXi


QuorumController.appendRecords =C2=A0(metadata/src/main/java/org/apache/kaf=
ka/controller/QuorumController.java:918-933) splits a non-atomic result int=
o batches, but its
boundary check is off by one. When records.size() is an exact multiple of m=
axRecordsPerBatch, the final iteration calls the appender with an empty sub=
List(size, size).
{code:java}
int startIndex =3D 0, numBatches =3D 0;
while (true) {
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 numBatches++;
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 int endIndex =3D startIndex + maxRecordsPerBatch;
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 if (endIndex > records.size()) {
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 long offset =3D appender.apply(records.subList(=
startIndex, records.size()));
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 ...
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 return offset;
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 } else {
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 =C2=A0 appender.apply(records.subList(startIndex, endI=
ndex));
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 }
=C2=A0 =C2=A0 startIndex +=3D maxRecordsPerBatch;
}{code}
The production appender passes this empty list to RaftClient.prepareAppend,=
 and KafkaRaftClient.append rejects empty batches with IllegalArgumentExcep=
tion
("Append failed because there are no records").

Since appendRecords only converts ApiException, the exception escapes the e=
vent handler and is treated as an unexpected fault, so the active controlle=
r renounces leadership and the client receives UnknownServerException.

The preceding full batches may already have been appended, so the change ca=
n take effect even though the client sees a failure.



