Hi Chia-Ping Tsai,
Thankyou very much for the review.

chia_00: the description "Workers running the old version will not
recognize schemas with the new logical type names and will treat them as
raw INT64, potentially causing data misinterpretation." seems to be wrong
since the old worker will throw a NoClassDefFoundError, right?

Ans :: Sorry for the confusion but I meant old worker in the sense of old
data plane but I totally agree that  if a connector/converter compiled
against the new connect-api (its bytecode references TimestampMicros) is
deployed on an old worker, the first reference to TimestampMicros.SCHEMA
links against the worker's old connect-api, which lacks the class =E2=86=92
NoClassDefFoundError. I have updated the KIP to reflect the same.

chia_01: does this KIP handle timestamps before 1970? The test plan
mentions negative values, but the KIP does not define how
fromLogical/toLogical convert them.

Ans :: : Yes, negative (pre-1970) values are handled correctly, but the KIP
currently only implies it via the test plan. The encoded long is a signed
count since the Unix epoch; negative =3D before 1970. The reason it works
cleanly is that Instant stores an epochSecond (which may be negative) and a
nanoOfSecond that is always in [0, 999_999_999] =E2=80=94 so getNano() is n=
ever
negative, even for pre-1970 instants. I have updated to KIP with same
including a sample worked pre-1970 example.

chia_02: I guess an old sink can use TimestampConverter to convert the new
types back to Timestamp to avoid compatibility issues? If so, it would be
nice to document that in the migration path.

Ans :: Yes totally agree and worth documenting.

I request you to please go through the update KIP once and review the
changes.

Thanks,
Pritam

